# 8. Distribution and commercial model

Trust should be acquired through institutions that customers already recognize.

## A. Priority channels

### Malaysia

- Islamic banks;
- Licensed digital investment and wealth platforms;
- Employers and payroll providers;
- Universities and professional associations;
- Takaful and retirement-planning partners; and
- Islamic-finance institutions with established customer trust.

### UAE and Gulf

- Islamic and conventional banks;
- Employers and end-of-service savings providers;
- Remittance companies;
- Payroll and wage platforms;
- Wealth managers;
- Telcos and digital wallets;
- Islamic-finance institutions; and
- Community or professional associations.

### Indonesia

- Banks and Islamic banks;
- Licensed wallets and payment providers;
- Telcos;
- Employers and payroll platforms;
- Cooperatives and approved savings groups;
- E-commerce ecosystems; and
- Remittance providers.

Any community or rotating-savings feature must be structured so that the app does not inadvertently become an unlicensed deposit-taker, lender, payment provider, or guarantor.

## B. Recommended commercial model

Start with B2B2C and embedded distribution:

- The regulated partner owns the customer relationship and custody;
- The startup charges a platform, software, or revenue-share fee;
- Any product-distribution compensation is disclosed;
- Recommendations cannot be ranked by commission;
- The customer can access the same product through the partner’s normal channels; and
- The startup has no incentive to maximize deposits or risk-taking at the expense of suitability.

A direct-to-consumer subscription may be added for budgeting and planning, but regulated investment revenue should remain transparent and partner-compliant.

## C. Local operating model

Each launch market should have:

- Local-language customer service;
- A local complaints address;
- Named compliance and risk ownership;
- Human escalation during relevant operating hours;
- Fraud response capability;
- Localized onboarding and disclosure testing; and
- At least one trusted institutional distribution partner.

A scholar or Islamic institution may support credibility, but endorsements should identify the precise product and opinion being endorsed. A general endorsement must not be presented as approval of every future AI recommendation.

