# 10. European-origin data, AI, and tax obligations

The company must maintain two compliance layers: European obligations and local-market obligations.

## A. European controls

The company should assess:

- GDPR controller/processor roles;
- Lawful basis for financial, identity, family, and religious-preference data;
- International-transfer mechanisms for data leaving the EEA;
- Data-processing agreements with cloud and AI providers;
- Data minimization and purpose limitation;
- Automated-decision and profiling rights;
- Retention and deletion rules;
- Security and breach-notification duties; and
- Whether the EU AI Act classifies any system as high-risk or imposes provider/deployer obligations.

Religious preference may constitute sensitive personal data under European data-protection rules. The company should not collect it unless necessary, with an appropriate legal basis and explicit, meaningful user control.

## B. Local data and retention

Before using European cloud infrastructure, confirm:

- Whether banking or financial data must remain locally hosted;
- Whether regulators require prior outsourcing approval;
- Whether cloud providers must meet local cybersecurity standards;
- Whether banking secrecy limits support-team access;
- Whether biometric or identity data has special restrictions;
- Whether deletion requests conflict with financial recordkeeping; and
- Whether model training on customer conversations is prohibited or requires consent.

The app should separate operational data, identity data, transaction records, recommendation records, and religious-preference settings. Financial records may need to be retained even when conversational data is deleted.

## C. Tax and reporting

The product must plan for:

- CRS and FATCA classification and reporting;
- Tax residency declarations;
- Withholding or reporting on investment income;
- Treatment of expatriates and dual residents;
- Currency conversion and gains;
- Employer or end-of-service contributions;
- Gift and contribution reporting;
- Cross-border remittance rules; and
- Tax treatment of charitable or purification payments.

The app should not provide tax advice unless it is licensed and qualified to do so. It should collect the minimum tax information necessary and explain why it is required.

---

# 11. Security, fraud, and financial controls

Required controls include:

- Local KYC and sanctions screening;
- Beneficial-owner verification;
- Device binding and session-risk monitoring;
- Strong authentication;
- Separate approval for withdrawals and investment changes;
- Cooling-off periods for unusual activity;
- New-beneficiary notifications;
- Transaction limits;
- Duplicate-payment prevention;
- Reconciliation between the app, payment provider, broker, custodian, and bank;
- SIM-swap and account-takeover controls;
- Clear lost-device recovery;
- Human fraud escalation; and
- A complete transaction and recommendation history.

No voice assistant or conversational AI should authorize a material withdrawal, investment, beneficiary change, or account closure without a second factor and written confirmation.

